11
00:34:26,086 --> 00:34:32,086
Halloween, is a celebration observed in many countries  on 31 October, the
eve of the Western Christian feast of All Hallows' Day.

12
00:37:33,913 --> 00:37:39,913
It is at the beginning of the observance of Allhallowtide, the time in the Christian liturgical year dedicated
to remembering the dead, including saints (hallows), martyrs, and all the faithful departed.

13
00:40:41,739 --> 00:40:47,739
In popular culture, Halloween has become a celebration of horror and
is associated with the macabre and the supernatural.

14
00:43:49,565 --> 00:43:55,565
One theory holds that many Halloween traditions were influenced by Celtic harvest festivals,
particularly the Gaelic festival Samhain, which are believed to have pagan roots.

15
00:46:57,391 --> 00:47:03,391
Some theories go further and suggest that Samhain may have been
Christianized as All Hallows' Day, along with its eve, by the Church.

16
00:50:05,217 --> 00:50:11,217
Other academics say Halloween began independently as a Christian
holiday, being the vigil  of All Hallows' Day.

17
00:53:13,043 --> 00:53:19,043
Celebrated in Ireland and Scotland for centuries, Irish and Scottish immigrants brought many Halloween customs to North America in the 19th
century, and then through American influence various Halloween customs spread to other countries by the late 20th and early 21st century.

18
00:56:20,869 --> 00:56:26,869
Popular activities during Halloween include trick-or-treating (or the related guising and souling), attending Halloween costume parties, carving pumpkins or turnips into jack-o'-lanterns,
lighting bonfires, apple bobbing, divination games, playing pranks, visiting haunted attractions, telling frightening stories, and watching horror or Halloween-themed films.

19
00:59:28,695 --> 00:59:34,695
Some Christians practice the observances of All Hallows' Eve, including attending church services and
lighting candles on the graves of the dead, although it is a secular celebration for others.

20
01:02:36,521 --> 01:02:42,521
Historically, some Christians abstained from meat on All Hallows' Eve, a tradition reflected in the eating
of certain vegetarian foods on this day, including apples, potato pancakes, and soul cakes.

21
